body:
**[comments] Rule 2, restates identifiers: shorten**

"One element is read through at(token, dim)" restates the at() method. "Views come only from an owner" restates the private constructor and the friend k_vnni_tensor. "A writable view converts to a read-only one, never the reverse" restates the requires clause of the converting constructor. The list of absent members (no pointer constructor, no data(), no operator[], no conversion) is visible in the struct body. The two facts that stay are the no-element-write rule and why there is no pointer access.

Proposed:
```cpp
// A packed K plane of [Rows tokens * Cols dimensions]. There is no element write:
// rows enter only through store_row and copy_token. There is no public pointer
// access: a bare pointer does not say which layout it points at.
```

Line-to-code mapping (Rule 4):
line 1 (the plane shape, no element write) -> template <typename T, size_t Rows, size_t Cols> and the single const accessor at(); line 2 (rows enter through store_row and copy_token) -> friend struct detail::k_vnni_access, the route those operations use; line 3 (no pointer access) -> private explicit k_vnni_view(T* data)

